Title = {Spatial modeling of ambient PM2.5 and population
   exposure in Addis Ababa using land use regression},
Journal = {ATMOSPHERIC ENVIRONMENT-X},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {31},
Month = {AUG},
Abstract = {Rapid urban growth in Africa is worsening air pollution in many cities,
   yet limited air-quality monitoring infrastructure hinders accurate
   assessment of residents' exposure. In this study, we applied a land-use
   regression (LUR) model to estimate annual ambient fine particulate
   matter (PM2.5) concentrations and assess population exposure in Addis
   Ababa, Ethiopia. Estimated annual PM2.5 concentrations showed
   substantial spatial variability, ranging from 5.9 to 47.9 \& micro;g/m3,
   with a citywide average of 17.3 \& micro;g/m3. No part of the city met
   the World Health Organization (WHO) annual air quality guideline of 5 \&
   micro;g/m3, and 77\% of the area exceeded Interim Target 3 (IT3; 15 \&
   micro;g/m3). The highest concentrations, exceeding the lowest threshold
   (IT-1, 35 \& micro;g/m3), were observed in the sub-cities of Arada,
   Addis Ketema, Lideta, and Kirkos, where dense urban development and
   major transportation corridors are concentrated. The LUR model
   demonstrated strong performance, with an adjusted R2 of 0.74 and a root
   mean square error (RMSE) of 1.85 \& micro;g/m3. Key predictors included
   proximity to waste dump sites, green space coverage, and the lengths of
   primary and secondary roads, reflecting major pollution sources and
   land-use patterns associated with reduced emissions or improved
   pollutant dispersion. Model diagnostics showed good internal validity,
   with low multicollinearity among predictors and no evidence of spatial
   autocorrelation in the residuals. We further quantified population
   exposure to PM2.5 by calculating population-weighted concentrations at
   both city and kebele (the lowest administrative unit) levels. The
   population-weighted citywide mean concentration was 21.3 \& micro;g/m3,
   while kebele-level exposures ranged from 13 to 30 \& micro;g/m3,
   highlighting significant disparities in pollution burden across the
   city. Overall, ambient PM2.5 concentrations substantially exceeded WHO
   guideline levels, underscoring the urgent need for stronger air quality
   policies, targeted emission reductions, and urban planning strategies to
   promote healthier, more equitable living environments.},
