@article{ WOS:001868062800003,
Author = {Gastrow, Claudia},
Title = {Conjuring geographies: creating `usable space' in urban Africa},
Journal = {AFRICA},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {96},
Number = {3},
Pages = {351-370},
Month = {AUG},
Abstract = {Over the past two decades, new high-end real estate developments have
   increasingly shaped Africa's urbanscapes. Scholars have generally
   criticized such projects, arguing that they do little to serve the needs
   of the broader population. In accounting for the investment in high-end
   real estate, the existing literature has focused on the domestic
   incentives that shape their development, the desires of local elites and
   bureaucracies, and international capital's search for new markets
   following the 2008 financial crisis. Interviews with investors suggest
   an alternative explanation: situated understandings of risk. Drawing on
   extended interviews with largely South African investors in urban real
   estate development and management in Africa, this article investigates
   how perceptions of risk, and the attempts to control it, shape
   investment. Faced with what they viewed as highly risky investment
   terrains, investors tried to recreate the financial environments they
   were familiar with through de-risking practices that sought to enclave
   projects from the geographies in which they were located. The result was
   investment in high-end real estate that was thought to be more
   controllable, calculable and, most of all, profitable. Enclaving is not
   just a growing characteristic of African urbanscapes; it is an
   underlying logic of the formal financial practices that produce them.
   Au cours des deux derni \& egrave;res d \& eacute;cennies, de nouveaux
   projets immobiliers haut de gamme ont profond \& eacute;ment transform
   \& eacute; les paysages urbains africains. Ces projets ont g \& eacute;n
   \& eacute;ralement \& eacute;t \& eacute; critiqu \& eacute;s par les
   chercheurs, estimant qu'ils ne r \& eacute;pondent gu \& egrave;re aux
   besoins de la population plus large. Pour expliquer les investissements
   dans l'immobilier haut de gamme, la litt \& eacute;rature existante
   s'est concentr \& eacute;e sur les incitations nationales qui fa \&
   ccedil;onnent leur d \& eacute;veloppement, les aspirations des \&
   eacute;lites et des administrations locales, ainsi que la qu \& ecirc;te
   de nouveaux march \& eacute;s par les capitaux internationaux apr \&
   egrave;s la crise financi \& egrave;re de 2008. Des entretiens avec des
   investisseurs sugg \& egrave;rent une autre explication : des interpr \&
   eacute;tations contextuelles du risque. S'appuyant sur des entretiens
   approfondis men \& eacute;s principalement aupr \& egrave;s
   d'investisseurs sud-africains actifs dans l'am \& eacute;nagement et la
   gestion de l'immobilier urbain en Afrique, cet article examine comment
   les imaginaires du risque et les tentatives de le ma \& icirc;triser
   influencent les investissements. Confront \& eacute;s \& agrave; des
   environnements d'investissement qu'ils jugeaient tr \& egrave;s risqu \&
   eacute;s, les investisseurs ont cherch \& eacute; \& agrave; recr \&
   eacute;er les environnements financiers qui leur \& eacute;taient
   familiers par des pratiques de r \& eacute;duction des risques visant \&
   agrave; isoler les projets des g \& eacute;ographies dans lesquelles ils
   sont implant \& eacute;s. Il en a r \& eacute;sult \& eacute; des
   investissements dans l'immobilier haut de gamme per \& ccedil;us comme
   plus contr \& ocirc;lables, plus pr \& eacute;visibles et, surtout, plus
   rentables. L'enclavement n'est pas seulement une caract \&
   eacute;ristique croissante des paysages urbains africains ; c'est une
   logique sous-jacente des pratiques financi \& egrave;res formelles qui
   les produisent.
   Ao longo das \& uacute;ltimas duas d \& eacute;cadas, os novos
   empreendimentos imobili \& aacute;rios de luxo t \& ecirc;m vindo a
   moldar cada vez mais a paisagem urbana de \& Aacute;frica. Os acad \&
   eacute;micos em geral, t \& ecirc;m criticado esses projetos,
   argumentando que pouco contribuem para satisfazer as necessidades da
   popula \& ccedil;\& atilde;o em geral. Ao analisar o investimento no
   setor imobili \& aacute;rio de luxo, a literatura existente tem-se
   centrado nos incentivos nacionais que moldam o seu desenvolvimento, nos
   desejos das elites e das burocracias locais e na procura de novos
   mercados por parte do capital internacional na sequ \& ecirc;ncia da
   crise financeira de 2008. Entrevistas com investidores sugerem uma
   explica \& ccedil;\& atilde;o alternativa: perce \& ccedil;\& otilde;es
   contextualizadas do risco. Com base em entrevistas aprofundadas com
   investidores, na sua maioria sul-africanos, no setor do desenvolvimento
   e gest \& atilde;o imobili \& aacute;ria urbana em \& Aacute;frica, este
   artigo investiga de que forma as perce \& ccedil;\& otilde;es do risco e
   as tentativas de o controlar moldam o investimento. Confrontados com o
   que consideravam mercados de investimento de alto risco, os investidores
   tentaram recriar os ambientes financeiros com os quais estavam
   familiarizados atrav \& eacute;s de pr \& aacute;ticas de redu \&
   ccedil;\& atilde;o do risco que visavam isolar os projetos das regi \&
   otilde;es geogr \& aacute;ficas onde se encontravam. O resultado foi o
   investimento em im \& oacute;veis de luxo, considerados mais control \&
   aacute;veis, previs \& iacute;veis e, acima de tudo, lucrativos. O
   enclausuramento n \& atilde;o \& eacute; apenas uma caracter \&
   iacute;stica crescente das paisagens urbanas africanas; \& eacute; uma l
   \& oacute;gica subjacente \& agrave;s pr \& aacute;ticas financeiras
   formais que as produzem.},
